<article class=3D"style_learnContent__K5K7M"><header><h1>Zero Striping</h1>=
</header><div class=3D"style_problemDescription__T4cPe"><p>For each zero in=
 an <code>m x n</code> matrix, set its entire row and column to zero <stron=
g>in place</strong>.</p><figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex;=
 justify-content: center;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a transformation of=
 a 4x5 matrix.  The initial matrix on the left displays numerical values fr=
om 1 to 19, arranged in a grid with rows labeled 0 to 3 and columns labeled=
 0 to 4.  One cell in row 1, column 1 contains a 0, highlighted in peach. T=
he bottom-right cell (row 3, column 4) also contains a 0, highlighted in li=
ght blue. A thick black arrow points to the right, indicating a transformat=
ion. The resulting matrix on the right shows the same numerical values, but=
 with specific cells replaced by 0s.  The cells in row 1, column 1; row 1, =
column 2; row 2, column 1; row 2, column 2; row 3, column 0; row 3, column =
1; row 3, column 2; row 3, column 3; and row 3, column 4 are all replaced w=
ith 0s.  These 0s are highlighted with peach, light blue, and light grey sh=
ading, indicating a pattern of replacement based on their position within t=
he original matrix.  The row and column labels remain consistent in both ma=
trices." width=3D"500" height=3D"190" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" sr=
c=3D"https://bytebytego.com/images/courses/coding-patterns/hash-maps-and-se=
ts/zero-striping/zero-striping-AFG7HJNI.svg" style=3D"color: transparent;">=
</div></figure></div>
<h2 id=3D"intuition---hash-sets">Intuition - Hash Sets</h2>
<p>A brute-force solution involves recording the positions of all 0s initia=
lly in the matrix and, for each of these 0s, iterating over their row and c=
olumn to set them to zero. However, imagine an input array that's filled wi=
th many zeros. In the worst case, iterating over every row and column for e=
ach zero will take <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math=
 xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><m=
o stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>=E2=8B=85</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretc=
hy=3D"false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</=
mo><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/=
x-tex">O(m\cdot n(m+n))</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=
=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"st=
rut" style=3D"height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class=3D"=
mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.02778em;">O</span><span class=3D"=
mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">m</span><span class=3D"mspac=
e" style=3D"margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class=3D"mbin">=E2=8B=85<=
/span><span class=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></spa=
n><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 1em; vertical=
-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span><span clas=
s=3D"mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">m</span><span class=3D"=
mspace" style=3D"margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class=3D"mbin">+</sp=
an><span class=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><=
span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 1em; vertical-al=
ign: -0.25em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span><span class=
=3D"mclose">))</span></span></span></span>, where <span class=3D"katex"><sp=
an class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML=
"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=E2=8B=85</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation=
 encoding=3D"application/x-tex">m \cdot n</annotation></semantics></math></=
span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><=
span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 0.4445em;"></span><span class=3D"mord=
 mathnormal">m</span><span class=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right: 0.2222em=
;"></span><span class=3D"mbin">=E2=8B=85</span><span class=3D"mspace" style=
=3D"margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class=3D"base"><span class=
=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnorma=
l">n</span></span></span></span> denotes the number of 0s, and (<span class=
=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/19=
98/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><anno=
tation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">m+n</annotation></semantics></math></=
span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><=
span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 0.6667em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"=
></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">m</span><span class=3D"mspace" styl=
e=3D"margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class=3D"mbin">+</span><span cla=
ss=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class=
=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 0.4306em;"></span><span cl=
ass=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span></span></span></span>) represents the total=
 number of cells in a row and column combined. This approach is quite ineff=
icient, so let=E2=80=99s look for a better solution.</p>
<p>Imagine any cell in the matrix. After the matrix is transformed, this ce=
ll will either retain its original value, or become zero. Is there a way to=
 tell if a cell is going to become zero?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The key observation is that if a cell is in a row or column containing a=
 zero, that cell will become zero.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>We could search a cell=E2=80=99s row and column to check if they contain=
 a zero, meaning each search would take <span class=3D"katex"><span class=
=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><seman=
tics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n=
</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"applicatio=
n/x-tex">O(m+n)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-=
html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=
=3D"height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathn=
ormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.02778em;">O</span><span class=3D"mopen">(</=
span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">m</span><span class=3D"mspace" style=
=3D"margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class=3D"mbin">+</span><span clas=
s=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class=3D=
"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;=
"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span><span class=3D"mclose">)</=
span></span></span></span> time. But it would be more efficient if we had a=
 way to check this in constant time, and this is where <strong>hash sets</s=
trong> would be useful. If we create two hash sets =E2=80=93 one to track a=
ll the rows containing a zero and another to track all the columns containi=
ng a zero =E2=80=93 we can determine if a specific cell's row or column con=
tains a zero in <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xm=
lns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo s=
tretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><anno=
tation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math><=
/span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base">=
<span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></spa=
n><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.02778em;">O</spa=
n><span class=3D"mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord">1</span><span class=3D=
"mclose">)</span></span></span></span> time.</p>
<p>With these hash sets created, the next step is to populate them. As we i=
terate through the matrix, when encountering a cell containing zero, we:</p=
>
<ul>
<li>Add its row index to the row hash set (<code>zero_rows</code>).</li>
